contract 1

[lane keys]
acceptance: required|none (default required): whether a work item on the lane must list an acceptance criterion before it runs
children_branch: string: the branch-name template the daemon checks a sub-ticket of this lane's work items out on — it must carry {seq} (the parent's seq), may carry {project} (the parent's project name), and its substituted name must be a legal git branch ref (WFC-112 otherwise)
contract: int, required, == ContractVersion
defaults: map {model_candidates, tiers}
description: string, required, non-empty
epic: bool (default false): the lane's own work items are epics — the lane must also declare children_branch and resume_reset (WFC-112 otherwise), and a first claim checks such a work item out on the children branch substituted with its own seq, records no fixed target (it merges into the origin's default branch) and snapshots its children
exclusive: bool (default false): one unfinished work item per project on the lane — a create on it reaches aihub exclusive, refused while another is not wrapped, failed or canceled
max_rewinds: int >= 0; required when any step declares on_fail, refused otherwise
name: string, required, ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,39}$, == directory name
resume_reset: string: the step id an owner-resumed epic's changed-children marker resets the lane to — required when the lane declares epic, refused without it, and it must name a step of this lane (WFC-112 otherwise)
steps: []step, required, non-empty
uncounted_rewinds: map {reason_contains, max}: the lane-declared class of gate-FAIL rewinds charged to no budget — a gate FAIL whose one-line hand-off reason carries reason_contains rewinds without spending max_rewinds, counted on its own toward max (the default 2 when the lane declares none; 0 = every matching FAIL asks the owner first, exactly like a spent max_rewinds); allowed only when a step declares on_fail, refused otherwise

[defaults keys]
model_candidates: []<harness>:<model>[:<effort>]
tiers: map <tier> -> []<harness>:<model>[:<effort>]; the table a step's model_tier names

[step keys]
budget: map {wall_minutes, tool_minutes}
fast_check_proof: map {result, command} (default none): the fast-check proof this step's done needs for each task repo it changed — a `pf-step-result/1` file with verdict pass whose heads carry the repo at its current HEAD; a repo the step did not change and a fast-forward need none
gate: bool
hub: map {verbs, attrs} (default none): what the step may call through `pf step hub`
human_mergeable: bool (default false): a project may declare the step's work done by a person with `steps.<project>.<step>.by: human` in its workspace yaml, fixed when the work item is first claimed; the step then asks the owner to do its work instead of running it itself, and a claim of a lane that does not declare the step human_mergeable is refused naming it
id: string, required, ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,39}$, unique
model_candidates: []<harness>:<model>[:<effort>]; exclusive with model_tier
model_tier: a tier of defaults.tiers; exclusive with model_candidates
on_fail: map {target}, gate only
params: map <name> -> a single-line string (the lane default): the step's declared parameters. <name> is ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$, collides with no var or fragment (WFC-195), and a parameter the definition's placeholders never use is allowed (a script reads it); a value is one line of at most 256 bytes (WFC-112). The final value is composed at EVERY dispatch — lane default < the project's steps.<project>.<step-id>.params.<name> in its workspace yaml < the work item's pf override — fills the definition's {{<name>}} and reaches the step's scripts as PF_PARAM_<NAME>
protected_stop: bool (default false): the step's `pf step ask` question is a human stop the daemon's automatic reply never answers (polyforge#239) — for an owner-only stop a gate flag cannot express (a hold only the owner's word clears, an owner-ruled revert). A gate is protected without the key; a step of a lane this build cannot verify is protected too
resources: map {admission, locks}: what the daemon takes for the step's dispatch
rewind_only: bool (default false): the step runs only after a rewind covers it — the first round records it skipped without a dispatch, and it runs once any rewind that covers it (a gate FAIL's or an owner's) resets it to pending; a project skip of a `skippable` it declares wins over it, and the loader refuses the declaration when no gate's [on_fail target, gate) range covers the step (WFC-112)
skill: skill name: <lane>/skills/<name>.md, else _shared/skills/<name>.md, rendered into the prompt
skippable: bool (default false): a project may switch the step off with `steps.<project>.<step>.skip: true` in its workspace yaml, fixed when the work item is first claimed; a switched-off step is never dispatched (its execution status is skipped), and the wrap_attrs of skipped steps at the lane's end pass to the last step that runs
vars: map[string]string
vars_files: map <var> -> a file of the lane (fragments/<name>.md), relative to the lane directory and inside it: the var's value is the file's text, exactly as if written in vars; a var is declared in vars or in vars_files, not both
waivable: bool (default false), gate only: the gate may hand off waive on the owner's word
wrap_attrs: []attr name (^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$), each at most once (default none): the wrap attrs the step registers (`pf step hub wrap-attrs <name>`) before its round may hand off done; listing one lets the step call wrap-attrs. When the steps at the lane's end are skipped (a skippable step a project switched off), their wrap_attrs are the last running step's to register

[fast_check_proof keys]
command: string, required: one line carrying {repo} (WFC-112 otherwise): the command a refusal quotes to write a fresh proof
result: string, required: the result file's path relative to the capsule, carrying {repo} (WFC-112 otherwise)

[on_fail keys]
target: an earlier step id, required

[resources keys]
admission: none|heavy (default none): a heavy-run admission slot for the dispatch
locks: []merge: the task repos' per-repo merge locks

[hub keys]
attrs: []attrs key (^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$), each at most once (default none): the top-level attrs keys the step may write (`pf step hub attrs <wi> --set <key>`); listing one lets the step call attrs, and the daemon refuses a write of any other key
verbs: []get|list|file|wrap-attrs|batch, each at most once (default none): the `pf step hub` verbs the step may call

[budget keys]
tool_minutes: int > 0, <= 180, <= wall_minutes when both are declared: the step's own tool window
wall_minutes: int > 0

[definition keys]
description: string, required, non-empty
name: string, required, == file name

[skill keys]
commands: []string, required (may be empty): the pre-written commands the skill runs by name
description: string, required, non-empty
name: string, required, == file name

[removed keys]
definition.output: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the output schema; the step hands off with `pf step handoff`, and its skill (or this definition) says what the hand-off carries
on_fail.charge: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; a FAIL is charged to max_rewinds unless the gate hands it off with --per-owner (the owner's word)
step.attrs: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key, its material (attrs/<name>.md or _shared/attr_<name>.md) and the workspace yaml's steps.<project>.<step>.attrs overrides
step.effects: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; the step registers its aihub writes with `pf step hub <verb>`, and its `hub:` names the verbs it may call and the attrs keys it may write
step.handoff: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; the step registers the record its wrap carries with `pf step hub wrap-attrs <name> @<file>` and declares `wrap_attrs: [<name>]` (its done is refused until the record is registered)
step.inputs: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; every step reads artifacts/handoff.md first, and its skill (or definition) names the files it reads before it starts
step.outputs: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; the step hands off with `pf step handoff` (a body, the --evidence files), and its skill says what the hand-off carries
step.verdicts: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; a gate hands off done (its pass) or fail, and a verdict a later command needs is in that command's result file
step.verifies: removed before workflow contract 1: delete the key; the step's skill says what it reads and checks of its upstream before it starts

[properties]
acceptance: whether a work item on the lane must list an acceptance criterion (`## Acceptance` or `## 验收`) before it runs (absent = required; none = the lane has no criterion to list): pf submit refuses an item without one only on a lane that requires it, the daemon's auto claim skips such an item only there, pf queue (and the Pi Web list, which reads its acceptance_missing) marks it only there, and pf file create prints the carried-by hint only there; the pre-submit analysis check (a `## 分析` / `## Analysis` section with content) applies on the same lanes — refused by pf submit, skipped by the auto claim, marked 需要交互：未分析 by pf queue; a lane the reader cannot load counts as required; a queued or blocked execution ticket's analysis can be re-scheduled with `pf file analysis redo`, the old `## 分析` text kept verbatim in `## 历次分析` and the new draft unconfirmed again, with the strong guarantee local to that daemon
children_branch: the branch-name template the daemon's claim of a sub-ticket reads from the PARENT's lane (the lane of the work item its `parent_id` names — never the sub-ticket's own): {seq} is the parent's seq and {project} its project name; the sub-ticket's first claim fixes the substituted branch into runtime-state/target-branch.json before the task repos are cloned, every (re-)claim checks the fixed value out (the carrier restore included) and the daemon creates the branch on each origin from the default branch tip with a non-forced push when the remote does not carry it; a claim that cannot read the parent or its lane is refused naming why; a parent whose lane declares none leaves the sub-ticket untouched
epic: the lane's own work items are epics: the lane must also declare children_branch and resume_reset (the loader refuses the key without them, WFC-112); a first claim fixes the children branch substituted with the work item's own seq into runtime-state/target-branch.json as the record's checkout key — no target key, so every reader keeps its default-branch logic and the epic merges into the origin's default branch — and snapshots the item's children into runtime-state/children.json, which the driver re-checks against aihub before every dispatch; a sub-ticket's claim reads the parent's lane exactly as before
exclusive: one unfinished work item per project on the lane: the daemon's data-plane proxy reads it in the lane set of the project's workspace and forwards a work-item create on the lane (`pf file create --bundle <lane>`) to aihub exclusive, refused CORE-409 OPEN_ON_BUNDLE while another work item of the project on the lane is not wrapped, failed or canceled; nothing else reads it
resume_reset: the step id an owner-resumed epic's changed-children marker (runtime-state/epic-resume.json) resets the lane to — required when the lane declares epic and refused without it (WFC-112), and it must name a step of this lane: the runtime writes it into the marker and the driver lands it as one owner-class rewind, charged to no budget; the Go code names no step id (workflow contract ⑥)

[placeholders]
{{<var>}}: the step's vars (vars, vars_files)
{{<param>}}: the step's params (params), at the dispatch's final value
{{<fragment>}}: _shared/<fragment>.md, inserted verbatim

[error codes]
WFC-100: `contract` missing
WFC-101: `contract` is not the version this daemon implements, or the lane is a workflow.md (the lane file of the earlier releases)
WFC-110: workflow.yaml missing, unparsable or not one mapping, a duplicate key, or a workflow.md beside it
WFC-111: unknown or removed key (at any level)
WFC-112: a value of the wrong type or outside its range
WFC-113: a required key is missing
WFC-120: `name` malformed or not the lane's directory name
WFC-121: `description` missing or empty
WFC-122: a user lane named like a built-in lane of its lane set (it never shadows the built-in lane)
WFC-123: (claim) the work item's lane is not in its workspace's lane set (the built-in lanes and that workspace's user lanes); the work item stays queued
WFC-130: step id malformed or duplicated
WFC-140: `on_fail` on a non-gate step, or a target that is not an earlier step
WFC-190: a step's definition file is missing
WFC-191: a definition file's frontmatter is missing, unparsable, carries an unknown key, or its `name`/`description` is wrong
WFC-195: a placeholder no var, param or fragment resolves; a var nothing uses; a name held by both a var and a param, or a var or param shadowing a fragment
WFC-198: a model candidate is not `<harness>:<model>[:<effort>]`, or `model_tier` names no tier of `defaults.tiers`
WFC-200: `skill` resolves to no file (<lane>/skills/<name>.md, else _shared/skills/<name>.md), or its frontmatter is not {name == the file name, description, commands: []string} over a non-empty body

[required]
lane.contract
lane.description
lane.name
lane.steps
step.id
fast_check_proof.command
fast_check_proof.result
on_fail.target
definition.description
definition.name
skill.commands
skill.description
skill.name

[values]
lane.acceptance: none
lane.acceptance: required
resources.admission: none
resources.admission: heavy
resources.locks: merge
hub.verbs: get
hub.verbs: list
hub.verbs: file
hub.verbs: wrap-attrs
hub.verbs: batch
